{"task_id": "HumanEval/33", "solution": "```python\ndef sort_third(l: list):\n    \"\"\"This function takes a list l and returns a list l' such that\n    l' is identical to l in the indicies that are not divisible by three, while its values at the indicies that are divisible by three are equal\n    to the values of the corresponding indicies of l, but sorted.\n    >>> sort_third([1, 2, 3])\n    [1, 2, 3]\n    >>> sort_third([5, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 2])\n    [2, 6, 3, 4, 8, 9, 5]\n    \"\"\"\n    if not l:\n        return []\n\n    third_elements = []\n    third_indices = []\n\n    # Extract values and store indices for elements divisible by three\n    for i in range(len(l)):\n        if i % 3 == 0:\n            third_elements.append(l[i])\n            third_indices.append(i)\n\n    # Sort the extracted values\n    third_elements.sort()\n\n    # Create a new list to store the result\n    result_list = l[:]\n\n    # Replace the elements in the new list with the sorted values\n    for i, original_index in enumerate(third_indices):\n        result_list[original_index] = third_elements[i]\n\n    return result_list\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/49", "solution": "def modp(n: int, p: int):\n    \"\"\"Return 2^n modulo p (be aware of numerics).\n    >>> modp(3, 5)\n    3\n    >>> modp(1101, 101)\n    2\n    >>> modp(0, 101)\n    1\n    >>> modp(3, 11)\n    8\n    >>> modp(100, 101)\n    1\n    \"\"\"\n    # Handle the special case where the modulus is 1, as x % 1 is always 0.\n    # This correctly covers cases like modp(0, 1) as per design context tests.\n    if p == 1:\n        return 0\n\n    result = 1\n    # Initialize base as 2 modulo p to prevent intermediate values from growing too large.\n    base = 2 % p\n\n    # Implement binary exponentiation (exponentiation by squaring).\n    while n > 0:\n        if n % 2 == 1:\n            result = (result * base) % p\n        base = (base * base) % p\n        n //= 2\n    return result"}
