{"task_id": "HumanEval/64", "solution": "```python\ndef vowels_count(s):\n    \"\"\"Write a function vowels_count which takes a string representing\n    a word as input and returns the number of vowels in the string.\n    Vowels in this case are 'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'. Here, 'y' is also a\n    vowel, but only when it is at the end of the given word.\n\n    Example:\n    >>> vowels_count(\"abcde\")\n    2\n    >>> vowels_count(\"ACEDY\")\n    3\n    \"\"\"\n    VOWELS_SET = {'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u'}\n    count = 0\n    s_lower = s.lower()\n\n    for i, char in enumerate(s_lower):\n        if char in VOWELS_SET:\n            count += 1\n        elif char == 'y' and i == len(s_lower) - 1:\n            count += 1\n    return count\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/65", "solution": "```python\ndef circular_shift(x, shift):\n    \"\"\"Circular shift the digits of the integer x, shift the digits right by shift\n    and return the result as a string.\n    If shift > number of digits, return digits reversed.\n    >>> circular_shift(12, 1)\n    \"21\"\n    >>> circular_shift(12, 2)\n    \"12\"\n    \"\"\"\n    s = str(x)\n    is_negative = x < 0\n\n    if is_negative:\n        sign = s[0]\n        digits_str = s[1:]\n    else:\n        sign = \"\"\n        digits_str = s\n\n    num_actual_digits = len(digits_str)\n\n    # As per FR3: If shift > number of digits, return digits reversed.\n    # The 'number of digits' refers to the numerical digits, excluding any sign.\n    if shift > num_actual_digits:\n        shifted_digits = digits_str[::-1]\n    else:\n        # As per FR4: Perform a circular right shift.\n        effective_shift = shift % num_actual_digits\n        if effective_shift == 0:\n            shifted_digits = digits_str\n        else:\n            shifted_digits = digits_str[-effective_shift:] + digits_str[:-effective_shift]\n\n    return sign + shifted_digits\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/68", "solution": "```python\ndef pluck(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the smallest even-valued node and its index in an array, prioritizing smaller indices for ties.\n    Returns [smallest_value, its_index] or [] if no even values or the array is empty.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return []\n\n    smallest_even_value = float('inf')\n    smallest_even_index = -1\n\n    for index, value in enumerate(arr):\n        if value % 2 == 0:  # Check if the value is even\n            if value < smallest_even_value:\n                smallest_even_value = value\n                smallest_even_index = index\n            elif value == smallest_even_value and index < smallest_even_index:\n                # If same smallest even value, pick the one with the smallest index\n                smallest_even_index = index\n\n    if smallest_even_index != -1:\n        return [smallest_even_value, smallest_even_index]\n    else:\n        return []\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/67", "solution": "```python\ndef fruit_distribution(s,n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Parses a string for apple and orange counts, then calculates the number of mangoes.\n    Assumes valid input format and handles calculations based on given examples.\n    \"\"\"\n    import re\n\n    # Regex to find numbers before \"apples\" and \"oranges\"\n    # Example: \"5 apples and 6 oranges\" -> group 1: \"5\", group 2: \"6\"\n    match = re.search(r\"(\\d+)\\s*apples\\s*and\\s*(\\d+)\\s*oranges\", s)\n\n    # Extract apple and orange counts and convert to integers\n    # Based on the problem description and examples, we assume valid input string format.\n    apples = int(match.group(1))\n    oranges = int(match.group(2))\n\n    # Calculate the number of mangoes\n    mangoes = n - (apples + oranges)\n\n    return mangoes\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/70", "solution": "```python\ndef strange_sort_list(lst):\n    '''\n    Given list of integers, return list in strange order.\n    Strange sorting, is when you start with the minimum value,\n    then maximum of the remaining integers, then minimum and so on.\n\n    Examples:\n    strange_sort_list([1, 2, 3, 4]) == [1, 4, 2, 3]\n    strange_sort_list([5, 5, 5, 5]) == [5, 5, 5, 5]\n    strange_sort_list([]) == []\n    '''\n    if not lst:\n        return []\n\n    sorted_list = sorted(lst)\n    result = []\n    is_min_turn = True\n\n    while sorted_list:\n        if is_min_turn:\n            result.append(sorted_list.pop(0)) # Smallest remaining\n        else:\n            result.append(sorted_list.pop()) # Largest remaining\n        is_min_turn = not is_min_turn\n    return result\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/69", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef search(lst):\n    '''\n    You are given a non-empty list of positive integers. Return the greatest integer that is greater than\n    zero, and has a frequency greater than or equal to the value of the integer itself.\n    The frequency of an integer is the number of times it appears in the list.\n    If no such a value exist, return -1.\n    Examples:\n        search([4, 1, 2, 2, 3, 1]) == 2\n        search([1, 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 4, 4]) == 3\n        search([5, 5, 4, 4, 4]) == -1\n    '''\n    frequencies = Counter(lst)\n\n    qualifying_integers = []\n    for num, freq in frequencies.items():\n        if freq >= num:\n            qualifying_integers.append(num)\n\n    if not qualifying_integers:\n        return -1\n    else:\n        return max(qualifying_integers)\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/71", "solution": "import math\n\ndef triangle_area(a, b, c):\n    '''\n    Given the lengths of the three sides of a triangle. Return the area of\n    the triangle rounded to 2 decimal points if the three sides form a valid triangle.\n    Otherwise return -1\n    Three sides make a valid triangle when the sum of any two sides is greater\n    than the third side.\n    '''\n    # Validate if the three sides form a valid triangle and are positive.\n    if not (a > 0 and b > 0 and c > 0 and\n            a + b > c and a + c > b and b + c > a):\n        return -1.0\n\n    # Calculate the semi-perimeter.\n    s = (a + b + c) / 2\n\n    # Calculate the area using Heron's formula.\n    # Use max(0, ...) to handle potential floating point inaccuracies that might result in a slightly negative value.\n    area_squared = s * (s - a) * (s - b) * (s - c)\n    area = math.sqrt(max(0, area_squared))\n\n    # Round the area to 2 decimal places.\n    return round(area, 2)"}
