{"task_id": "HumanEval/118", "solution": "def get_closest_vowel(word):\n    \"\"\"You are given a word. Your task is to find the closest vowel that stands between\n    two consonants from the right side of the word (case sensitive).\n\n    Vowels in the beginning and ending doesn't count. Return empty string if you didn't\n    find any vowel met the above condition.\n\n    You may assume that the given string contains English letter only.\n\n    Example:\n    get_closest_vowel(\"yogurt\") ==> \"u\"\n    get_closest_vowel(\"FULL\") ==> \"U\"\n    get_closest_vowel(\"quick\") ==> \"\"\n    get_closest_vowel(\"ab\") ==> \"\"\n    \"\"\"\n    vowels = {'a', 'e', 'i', 'o', 'u', 'A', 'E', 'I', 'O', 'U'}\n    n = len(word)\n\n    # A vowel needs to be surrounded by two consonants (CVC), so the minimum word length is 3.\n    # The vowel cannot be the first or last character, hence its index `i` must be between 1 and n-2.\n    if n < 3:\n        return \"\"\n\n    # Iterate from right to left, starting from the second-to-last character down to the second character.\n    # This range ensures `word[i-1]` and `word[i+1]` are valid indices.\n    for i in range(n - 2, 0, -1):\n        current_char = word[i]\n\n        # Check if the current character is a vowel\n        if current_char in vowels:\n            prev_char = word[i - 1]\n            next_char = word[i + 1]\n\n            # A character is a consonant if it is an English letter and not a vowel.\n            # We assume all characters are English letters as per the problem description.\n            is_prev_consonant = prev_char not in vowels\n            is_next_consonant = next_char not in vowels\n\n            # If the current character is a vowel surrounded by two consonants, return it.\n            if is_prev_consonant and is_next_consonant:\n                return current_char\n\n    # If no such vowel is found after checking all valid positions, return an empty string.\n    return \"\""}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/119", "solution": "```python\ndef match_parens(lst):\n    '''\n    You are given a list of two strings, both strings consist of open\n    parentheses '(' or close parentheses ')' only.\n    Your job is to check if it is possible to concatenate the two strings in\n    some order, that the resulting string will be good.\n    A string S is considered to be good if and only if all parentheses in S\n    are balanced. For example: the string '(())()' is good, while the string\n    '())' is not.\n    Return 'Yes' if there's a way to make a good string, and return 'No' otherwise.\n    '''\n    def is_balanced(s: str) -> bool:\n        \"\"\"Checks if a string of parentheses is balanced.\"\"\"\n        count = 0\n        for char in s:\n            if char == '(':\n                count += 1\n            elif char == ')':\n                count -= 1\n            if count < 0:\n                return False\n        return count == 0\n\n    s1, s2 = lst[0], lst[1]\n\n    if is_balanced(s1 + s2):\n        return 'Yes'\n\n    if is_balanced(s2 + s1):\n        return 'Yes'\n\n    return 'No'\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/125", "solution": "def split_words(txt):\n    '''\n    Given a string of words, return a list of words split on whitespace, if no whitespaces exists in the text you\n    should split on commas ',', if no commas exists you should return the number of lower-case letters with odd order in the\n    alphabet, ord('a') = 0, ord('b') = 1, ... ord('z') = 25\n    Examples\n    split_words(\"Hello world!\") \u279e [\"Hello\", \"world!\"]\n    split_words(\"Hello,world!\") \u279e [\"Hello\", \"world!\"]\n    split_words(\"abcdef\") == 3\n    '''\n    if not isinstance(txt, str):\n        raise TypeError(\"Input must be a string.\")\n\n    # Check for whitespace\n    has_whitespace = any(c.isspace() for c in txt)\n    if has_whitespace:\n        return txt.split()\n\n    # Check for commas\n    has_comma = ',' in txt\n    if has_comma:\n        return txt.split(',')\n\n    # Count odd-ordered lowercase letters\n    count = 0\n    ALPHABET_OFFSET = ord('a')\n    for char in txt:\n        if 'a' <= char <= 'z':\n            order = ord(char) - ALPHABET_OFFSET\n            if order % 2 != 0:\n                count += 1\n    return count"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/123", "solution": "```python\ndef get_odd_collatz(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Given a positive integer n, return a sorted list that has the odd numbers in collatz sequence.\n\n    The Collatz conjecture is a conjecture in mathematics that concerns a sequence defined\n    as follows: start with any positive integer n. Then each term is obtained from the\n    previous term as follows: if the previous term is even, the next term is one half of\n    the previous term. If the previous term is odd, the next term is 3 times the previous\n    term plus 1. The conjecture is that no matter what value of n, the sequence will always reach 1.\n\n    Note:\n        1. Collatz(1) is [1].\n        2. returned list sorted in increasing order.\n\n    For example:\n    get_odd_collatz(5) returns [1, 5] # The collatz sequence for 5 is [5, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1], so the odd numbers are only 1, and 5.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Custom exception for invalid input, to align with design principles if we were to include it.\n    # However, for a \"bare function\" output, we will rely on implicit errors or assume valid input\n    # as the problem statement doesn't specify error handling, only the design document.\n    # class InvalidInputError(ValueError):\n    #     \"\"\"Custom exception for invalid input parameters.\"\"\"\n    #     pass\n\n    # Input validation as per design, though omitted in final bare function to meet strict output format.\n    # if not isinstance(n, int) or n <= 0:\n    #     raise InvalidInputError(\"Input 'n' must be a positive integer.\")\n\n    current_term = n\n    odd_numbers = set()\n\n    # We use a set to track seen numbers to handle potential cycles,\n    # though for the Collatz conjecture, it's expected to always reach 1.\n    # This also naturally handles the case of n=1 where the sequence is just [1].\n    seen_terms = set()\n\n    while current_term not in seen_terms:\n        seen_terms.add(current_term)\n\n        if current_term % 2 != 0:\n            odd_numbers.add(current_term)\n\n        if current_term == 1:\n            break\n        elif current_term % 2 == 0:\n            current_term //= 2\n        else:\n            current_term = (3 * current_term) + 1\n\n    return sorted(list(odd_numbers))\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/124", "solution": "```python\ndef valid_date(date):\n    \"\"\"You have to write a function which validates a given date string and\n    returns True if the date is valid otherwise False.\n    The date is valid if all of the following rules are satisfied:\n    1. The date string is not empty.\n    2. The number of days is not less than 1 or higher than 31 days for months 1,3,5,7,8,10,12. And the number of days is not less than 1 or higher than 30 days for months 4,6,9,11. And, the number of days is not less than 1 or higher than 29 for the month 2.\n    3. The months should not be less than 1 or higher than 12.\n    4. The date should be in the format: mm-dd-yyyy\n\n    for example:\n    valid_date('03-11-2000') => True\n\n    valid_date('15-01-2012') => False\n\n    valid_date('04-0-2040') => False\n\n    valid_date('06-04-2020') => True\n\n    valid_date('06/04/2020') => False\n    \"\"\"\n    if not date:\n        return False\n\n    parts = date.split('-')\n    if len(parts) != 3:\n        return False\n\n    month_str, day_str, year_str = parts\n\n    if len(month_str) != 2 or len(day_str) != 2 or len(year_str) != 4:\n        return False\n\n    try:\n        month = int(month_str)\n        day = int(day_str)\n        year = int(year_str) # Year is not used for validation, but must be convertible\n    except ValueError:\n        return False\n\n    if not (1 <= month <= 12):\n        return False\n\n    if not (1 <= day):\n        return False\n\n    days_in_month = {\n        1: 31, 3: 31, 5: 31, 7: 31, 8: 31, 10: 31, 12: 31,\n        4: 30, 6: 30, 9: 30, 11: 30,\n        2: 29\n    }\n\n    max_days = days_in_month.get(month)\n    if max_days is None or day > max_days:\n        return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/126", "solution": "```python\nfrom collections import Counter\n\ndef is_sorted(lst):\n    '''\n    Given a list of numbers, return whether or not they are sorted\n    in ascending order. If list has more than 1 duplicate of the same\n    number, return False. Assume no negative numbers and only integers.\n    '''\n    if not lst:\n        return True\n\n    # Check for duplicate constraints.\n    counts = Counter(lst)\n    for num, count in counts.items():\n        if count > 2:\n            return False\n\n    # Check for ascending order.\n    for i in range(len(lst) - 1):\n        if lst[i] > lst[i+1]:\n            return False\n\n    return True\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/128", "solution": "```python\ndef prod_signs(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the sum of magnitudes multiplied by the product of signs of numbers in an array.\n    Returns None for an empty array.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not arr:\n        return None\n\n    sum_magnitudes = 0\n    product_signs = 1\n\n    for num in arr:\n        # Calculate magnitude (absolute value)\n        sum_magnitudes += abs(num)\n\n        # Determine sign\n        sign = 0\n        if num > 0:\n            sign = 1\n        elif num < 0:\n            sign = -1\n        # If num is 0, sign remains 0, ensuring product_signs becomes 0.\n\n        # Multiply signs\n        product_signs *= sign\n\n    return sum_magnitudes * product_signs\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/131", "solution": "```python\ndef digits(n):\n    \"\"\"Given a positive integer n, return the product of the odd digits.\n    Return 0 if all digits are even.\n    \"\"\"\n    product = 1\n    has_odd_digit = False\n\n    for digit_char in str(n):\n        digit = int(digit_char)\n        if digit % 2 != 0:  # Check if the digit is odd\n            product *= digit\n            has_odd_digit = True\n\n    if has_odd_digit:\n        return product\n    else:\n        return 0\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/133", "solution": "import math\n\ndef sum_squares(lst):\n    \"\"\"You are given a list of numbers.\n    You need to return the sum of squared numbers in the given list,\n    round each element in the list to the upper int(Ceiling) first.\n    Examples:\n    For lst = [1,2,3] the output should be 14\n    For lst = [1,4,9] the output should be 98\n    For lst = [1,3,5,7] the output should be 84\n    For lst = [1.4,4.2,0] the output should be 29\n    For lst = [-2.4,1,1] the output should be 6\n\n\n    \"\"\"\n    total_sum = 0\n    for num in lst:\n        rounded_num = math.ceil(num)\n        total_sum += rounded_num * rounded_num\n    return total_sum"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/127", "solution": "def intersection(interval1, interval2):\n    \"\"\"Calculates the intersection of two intervals, determines its length, and checks if the length is a prime number.\"\"\"\n\n    def is_prime(n):\n        \"\"\"Checks if a number is prime.\"\"\"\n        if n <= 1:\n            return False\n        i = 2\n        while i * i <= n:\n            if n % i == 0:\n                return False\n            i += 1\n        return True\n\n    start1, end1 = interval1\n    start2, end2 = interval2\n\n    intersect_start = max(start1, start2)\n    intersect_end = min(end1, end2)\n\n    if intersect_start <= intersect_end:\n        intersection_length = intersect_end - intersect_start + 1\n    else:\n        intersection_length = 0\n\n    if is_prime(intersection_length):\n        return \"YES\"\n    else:\n        return \"NO\""}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/132", "solution": "```python\ndef is_nested(string):\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a string containing only square brackets has a valid subsequence\n    where at least one bracket is nested, identified by the '[[...]]' pattern.\n    \"\"\"\n    state = 0  # 0: initial, 1: found first '[', 2: found '[[', 3: found '[[...]' (first ']')\n\n    for char in string:\n        if char == '[':\n            if state == 0:\n                state = 1\n            elif state == 1:\n                state = 2  # Found '[['\n            elif state == 3:\n                state = 2  # After '[[...]', if another '[' appears, we are still nested.\n            # If state is 2 and char is '[', state remains 2 (e.g., '[[[' is still looking for ']]')\n        elif char == ']':\n            if state == 1:\n                state = 0  # Found '[]', this '[' cannot lead to '[[', so reset.\n            elif state == 2:\n                state = 3  # Found '[[...]', first ']' after '[['\n            elif state == 3:\n                return True # Found '[[...]]', second ']' after '[[...]'\n            # If state is 0 and char is ']', state remains 0 (']' alone is irrelevant)\n\n    return False\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/130", "solution": "```python\ndef tri(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the Tribonacci sequence based on the defined recurrence relations\n    and returns a list of the first n + 1 numbers.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input 'n' must be a non-negative integer.\")\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return [1.0]\n\n    sequence = [0.0] * (n + 1)\n    sequence[0] = 1.0\n    sequence[1] = 3.0\n\n    def _calculate_tri_even(k):\n        return 1.0 + k / 2.0\n\n    for k in range(2, n + 1):\n        if k % 2 == 0:  # k is even\n            sequence[k] = _calculate_tri_even(k)\n        else:  # k is odd\n            tri_k_minus_1 = sequence[k - 1]\n            tri_k_minus_2 = sequence[k - 2]\n            tri_k_plus_1 = _calculate_tri_even(k + 1)  # k+1 is always even if k is odd\n            sequence[k] = tri_k_minus_1 + tri_k_minus_2 + tri_k_plus_1\n\n    return sequence\n```"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/129", "solution": "def minPath(grid, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the lexicographically smallest path of length k in the grid.\n    Uses a Depth-First Search (DFS) approach, starting from each cell and pruning paths that are already larger than the current minimum.\n    \"\"\"\n    N = len(grid)\n    # Initialize min_path with values larger than any possible grid value to ensure the first valid path is accepted.\n    min_path = [float('inf')] * k\n\n    # Define possible movements: (row_change, col_change) for Right, Left, Down, Up.\n    DIRECTIONS = [(0, 1), (0, -1), (1, 0), (-1, 0)]\n\n    def _find_min_path_dfs(r, c, current_path):\n        nonlocal min_path\n\n        # If current_path is already longer than or lexicographically greater than min_path's prefix, prune.\n        if len(current_path) > len(min_path) or (len(current_path) <= len(min_path) and current_path > min_path[:len(current_path)]):\n            return\n\n        # If a path of length k is formed, compare and update min_path if current_path is smaller.\n        if len(current_path) == k:\n            if current_path < min_path:\n                min_path = list(current_path) # Deep copy the path\n            return\n\n        # Explore neighbors\n        for dr, dc in DIRECTIONS:\n            nr, nc = r + dr, c + dc\n\n            # Check if the neighbor is within grid bounds.\n            if 0 <= nr < N and 0 <= nc < N:\n                _find_min_path_dfs(nr, nc, current_path + [grid[nr][nc]])\n\n    # Start DFS from every cell in the grid.\n    for r_start in range(N):\n        for c_start in range(N):\n            _find_min_dfs(r_start, c_start, [grid[r_start][c_start]])\n\n    return min_path"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/134", "solution": "def check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(txt):\n    '''\n    Create a function that returns True if the last character\n    of a given string is an alphabetical character and is not\n    a part of a word, and False otherwise.\n    Note: \"word\" is a group of characters separated by space.\n\n    Examples:\n    check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(\"apple pie\") \u279e False\n    check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(\"apple pi e\") \u279e True\n    check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(\"apple pi e \") \u279e False\n    check_if_last_char_is_a_letter(\"\") \u279e False\n    '''\n    if not txt:\n        return False\n\n    last_char = txt[-1]\n\n    if not last_char.isalpha():\n        return False\n\n    # If it's the only character and it's a letter, it's not part of a word.\n    if len(txt) == 1:\n        return True\n\n    # Check the character before the last one\n    char_before_last = txt[-2]\n\n    # If the character before the last one is a space, then the last letter\n    # is not part of a word.\n    return char_before_last == ' '"}
{"task_id": "HumanEval/136", "solution": "def largest_smallest_integers(lst):\n    '''\n    Finds the largest negative integer and the smallest positive integer in a list.\n    Returns (largest_negative, smallest_positive), using None if no such integers exist.\n    '''\n    largest_neg = None\n    smallest_pos = None\n\n    negatives = [x for x in lst if x < 0]\n    positives = [x for x in lst if x > 0]\n\n    if negatives:\n        largest_neg = max(negatives)\n    if positives:\n        smallest_pos = min(positives)\n\n    return (largest_neg, smallest_pos)"}
